digraph "Create a high-level Azure cloud architecture diagram for an AI-powered Infrastructure Modernization Platform with MCP (Model Context \
Protocol) integration:
Core Components:

Primary Input Layer:

Legacy Infrastructure Repositories (600+ repos: 400 Terraform, 200 \
BICEP)
GitHub/Azure DevOps repositories
Source code and infrastructure-as-code files


AI Reference Knowledge Base (via MCP):

Confluence \
MCP Server (organizational standards, internal documentation)
PDF MCP Server (best practices guides, migration playbooks)
ServiceNow \
MCP Server (approved patterns, change management policies, CMDB)
Microsoft Well-Architected Framework MCP Server (Azure design principles)
\
Azure Verified Modules Documentation MCP Server (AVM standards and templates)
MCP Context Aggregation Service


AI Processing Engine:

\
Azure OpenAI Service (GPT-4) - code analysis and transformation
MCP Context Injection - enriches prompts with guidance from knowledge \
base
Azure Cognitive Services - document understanding and pattern recognition
Migration logic and transformation rules engine


\
Application/Orchestration Layer:

Azure Container Apps - migration orchestration service
API Management - service exposure and gateway
\
Workflow Engine - migration pipeline coordination
Validation and Quality Gates service


Data/State Layer:

Azure SQL Database - \
migration tracking, audit logs, metadata
Azure Storage Account (Blob) - staged repositories, transformation artifacts
Azure Cosmos \
DB - MCP context cache and session management
Azure Redis Cache - performance optimization


Output Layer:

Azure Verified Modules (\
AVM) compliant code
Transformed infrastructure code
Migration reports and documentation
Compliance validation results


Supporting \
Infrastructure:

Azure Monitor & Application Insights - observability and diagnostics
Azure Key Vault - secrets and credentials \
management
Azure Virtual Network - network isolation
Azure Private Endpoints - secure connectivity
Azure DevOps/GitHub Actions - \
CI/CD automation



Architecture Requirements:

Clearly distinguish PRIMARY INPUT (Terraform/BICEP repos) from REFERENCE SOURCES (\
Confluence, PDF, SNOW, WAF, AVM docs)
Show how MCP servers provide contextual guidance to Azure OpenAI during transformation
Display \
the flow: Legacy Code → AI Analysis (with MCP guidance) → AVM Output
Indicate how reference sources improve transformation quality \
and compliance
Show real-time MCP context retrieval during migration processing
Highlight the feedback loop where AI queries reference \
sources for decision-making

Visual Style:

Use official Azure icon set
Differentiate visually between:

PRIMARY DATA FLOW (bold/\
solid lines): Terraform/BICEP → Processing → AVM
REFERENCE/GUIDANCE FLOW (dashed lines): Knowledge sources → MCP → AI enrichment


\
Color-code layers: Input (blue), MCP/Reference (green), AI Processing (purple), Storage (orange), Output (red)
Use clear directional \
arrows with labels
Show MCP as middleware providing \"just-in-time\" context

Key Technical Details:

Mark MCP servers as \"read-only \
reference\" vs \"processing pipeline\"
Show how AI queries MCP knowledge base during each transformation
Indicate context enrichment: \"\
AI asks: 'What's the AVM pattern for this?' → MCP retrieves → AI applies\"
Display async patterns for reference data caching
\
Show phased rollout (Year 1: Terraform 400 repos, Year 2: BICEP 200 repos, Year 3: Custom AVM)

Layout Guidance:

Top section: Reference \
Knowledge Sources (Confluence, PDF, SNOW, WAF, AVM docs) connected via MCP
Center section: Main processing flow (Legacy Repos → \
AI + MCP → Transformed Code)
Bottom section: Azure infrastructure services (storage, monitoring, security)
Clearly show MCP as \
the \"brain/memory\" that AI consults during transformation" {
	graph [bb="0,0,25102,3778",
